Yes. The cleanest move is to treat each rhetorical failure as a general information-processing pathology. Then the human-language version, mathematical version, code version, and Pantheon version become different instantiations of the same operator.

Human form	Mathematical / statistical analog	Code / systems analog	Pantheon analog
Sophistry	Optimize persuasiveness subject to weak truth constraints: argmax_A P(accept | A) rather than argmax_H P(H | evidence)	Code engineered to pass the demo/tests rather than satisfy the actual specification	Map–territory substitution: optimize the representation’s apparent coherence rather than correspondence
Rationalization	Choose hypothesis after seeing outcome: H := argmax_H fit(H, observed outcome)	Write implementation first, then rewrite requirements to describe what it already does	Reverse Goal Seek: move the target until the arrow looks accurate
Motivated reasoning	Biased prior or asymmetric loss: P(H) / L(error) depends on whether conclusion is desirable	Search algorithm assigns hidden bonuses to preferred outputs	Weighted Mirror: evidence is reflected through unequal semantic weights
Cherry-picking	Condition on favorable subset S* ⊂ D; report f(D_S*)	Filter logs/tests until failures disappear	Selective Delta Capture: preserve confirming deltas, discard disconfirming ones
Special pleading	Piecewise rule with an unjustified exception: f(x)=rule(x), x≠me; exemption, x=me	if user == "me": bypass_validation()	Jurisdiction Leak: one node silently exempts itself from the ontology/rules applied elsewhere
Equivocation	Replace one variable with two hidden meanings: x₁ ≠ x₂ while notation pretends x₁=x₂	Reuse the same variable/interface name for different semantics or units	Handle Collision: identical semantic handle resolves to different latent objects
